<h2>WCAG contrast ratio calculator</h2><p>WCAG 2.2 uses relative luminance to compare a text color with its background. For Success Criterion 1.4.3, normal text needs at least <strong>4.5:1</strong> contrast and large-scale text needs at least <strong>3:1</strong>. Enhanced (AAA) text contrast uses 7:1 for normal text and 4.5:1 for large text. <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C Contrast (Minimum) guidance</a>.</p>
<h2>How contrast is calculated</h2><p>Each sRGB channel is linearized, weighted into relative luminance, and the lighter and darker luminances are used in <strong>(L1 + 0.05) ÷ (L2 + 0.05)</strong>. WCAG notes that threshold values should not be rounded upward for pass/fail decisions, so this tool compares the full precision value even though it displays two decimals.</p>
<h2>What counts as large text?</h2><p>Large-scale text is not simply anything that looks like a heading. WCAG defines it by rendered size and, for lower sizes, font weight. Check the current W3C definition for the exact CSS/point equivalents and do not apply the 3:1 threshold to ordinary body text.</p>
<h2>Contrast is only one accessibility check</h2><p>This calculator does not assess gradients, background images, transparency, hover/focus states, non-text contrast, font rendering or whether color is the only means of conveying information. Test the final rendered component and all interaction states.</p>
